--TEST--
a `Core` enum in front of a case answers the enum's own card — its declaration line, its sentence and one line per case, `rule:core-api/reference-card`
--FILE--
<?nvs
var $verb = Core\Http\Met<|>hod::Get;
echo $verb;
--REQUEST--
hover
--EXPECT--
```nvs
enum Core\Http\Method
```

The closed set of HTTP verbs a `#[Route]` may be declared under and a request may carry — eight of them, safe ones first so that the four the CSRF check covers are the contiguous tail from `Post` on; `CONNECT` is deliberately absent.

**Cases**

- `Get` — Reads a resource; safe, so no CSRF check applies.
- `Head` — `Get` without a response body; safe.
- `Options` — Asks what a resource supports; safe.
- `Trace` — Echoes the request back; safe.
- `Post` — Submits data and may change state; the first of the CSRF-covered tail.
- `Put` — Replaces a resource; CSRF-covered.
- `Patch` — Changes a resource in place; CSRF-covered.
- `Delete` — Removes a resource; CSRF-covered.
